 ### [MSE Loss and PSNR Conversion](1651-mse-loss-and-psnr-conversion.md)
-The Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss computes the average of the squared differences between predicted and target RGB values, serving as the primary photometric optimization objective in radiance-field training. It is often accompanied by the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), a logarithmic metric derived from MSE that quantifies reconstruction fidelity for logging and evaluation. Unlike structural metrics such as SSIM, MSE is sensitive to pixel-level intensity errors and large smooth regions, making it a standard baseline for differentiable volume rendering.
+The Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss computes the average of the squared differences between predicted and target RGB values, serving as the primary photometric fidelity metric for training differentiable volume rendering models. It is often accompanied by the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), a logarithmic transformation of MSE used for evaluation and logging, where higher values indicate better reconstruction quality. Unlike structural metrics such as SSIM, MSE focuses strictly on pixel-wise intensity differences, making it sensitive to large smooth regions but less responsive to small, high-frequency features.
 
 ### [L1+SSIM Loss](2890-l1-ssim-loss.md)
-L1+SSIM Loss is a composite photometric objective used to optimize radiance fields and 3D Gaussian splats by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground-truth photographs. It combines a pixel-wise L1 error term with a structural similarity (SSIM) term to balance color accuracy with perceptual sharpness. Unlike pure MSE losses which can produce blurry results, this method explicitly rewards matching local structures such as edges and textures.
+L1+SSIM Loss is a composite photometric objective used to optimize radiance fields and 3D Gaussian splats by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground-truth reference views. It combines a pixel-wise L1 error term with a Structural Similarity (SSIM) term to preserve local structures and edges while maintaining color accuracy. It is distinguished from pure MSE-based methods by its robustness to outliers and its ability to produce sharper details, and from visibility-based losses by its direct comparison of rendered and target pixel values without explicit occlusion reasoning.
 
 ### [Visibility-Based Photometric Loss](2917-visibility-based-photometric-loss.md)
 Visibility-Based Photometric Loss computes photometric error only on pixels identified as visible across source and target views. It differs from standard photometric losses by filtering out pixels where the squared difference between reprojected source depth and directly rendered target depth exceeds a threshold, preventing the model from fitting to occluded or transient geometry.
 
+### [Adaptive Photometric Loss with Thresholding](9354-adaptive-photometric-loss-with-thresholding.md)
+Adaptive Photometric Loss with Thresholding is a composite regularization loss designed to improve the convergence and geometric accuracy of neural radiance field (NeRF) optimization, specifically in mesh-based or grid-based variants. It addresses the ambiguity of volume rendering by combining a standard photometric term with a secondary term that normalizes the color error by the remaining opacity, effectively forcing the model to concentrate opacity on the object surface rather than distributing it along the ray. It differs from standard MSE or L1+SSIM losses by introducing a dynamic weighting scheme and a thresholding mechanism that activates the opacity-normalized term only after a significant portion of training, aligning with traditional NeRF principles during early stages.
