 ## Background
-The use of photometric losses is foundational to neural rendering, originating with the introduction of NeRF [S1]. Early works relied heavily on MSE loss for its simplicity and differentiability. Subsequent research introduced structural losses (SSIM) to address the blurriness associated with MSE [S2]. More recent approaches, particularly in dynamic scenes and multi-view consistency, have incorporated visibility checks and temporal consistency terms to handle occlusions and temporal flickering [S1, S2]. The specific "Consistency Filter" mentioned in [S1] is part of a point-based radiance field method that uses spherical harmonics and a coarse-to-fine strategy, distinguishing it from dense voxel or grid-based NeRFs.
+Photometric losses are foundational to differentiable rendering, originating from the need to supervise neural radiance fields (NeRF) and related implicit representations using sparse or dense multi-view images. The standard MSE loss was adopted early in NeRF [S1] due to its simplicity and differentiability. The introduction of SSIM addressed MSE's tendency to produce blurry results by incorporating structural information [S1]. Visibility-based losses emerged from multi-view stereo and SLAM literature, adapted to differentiable rendering to handle occlusions and dynamic scenes [S1, S2]. Adaptive and opacity-normalized losses were developed to resolve the "floaters" and transparency ambiguity issues in volume rendering, particularly in point-based or grid-based variants [S1]. Temporal consistency losses were introduced for dynamic scene reconstruction to ensure smooth transitions between frames [S2].
 
